174:## Rulings on R20 (order-preserving decimal encodings) (2026-11-07; Q35; lab/research/R20-order-preserving-decimal-encodings.md)
177:| Q35a encoding | The decimal order key is TDK1, determined by the value alone: zero `o`; positive `p` LL I F (LL two-digit integer-digit count, I without leading zeros, F without trailing zeros); negative `n` (99-L) comp(I) comp(F) `z` with comp(d) = 9-d. Alphabet `[0-9nopz]` only, so ICU order equals codepoint order on QLever and byte order on XTDB (no collation on 2.2.0). The contract's "fixed-width, sign-aware" wording becomes "value-determined, order-preserving (TDK1)". | R20 property test: 0 failures over 2.62M exhaustive values, 400k random and 100k adjacent pairs, 779 declarations, 600k decoder fuzz; 5/5 mutant encoders caught; ICU check 0 disagreements |
178:| Q35b one representation (amends Q18) | The decimal kind has one stored representation on both lanes: the TDK1 key (authoritative, decodes exactly) plus derived canonical text at the declared scale; QLever also keeps its lexical channel for parity with other kinds. Q18's "scaled integer when precision ≤ 15" no longer applies to the decimal kind; users who model money as integer cents use the integer kind. Because TDK1 depends on the value only, any widening that admits every existing value is metadata-only (Q31g's key-compatibility condition always holds); narrowing still validates every value and fails atomically. Comparison across attributes of different declared scale is exact. | R20 section on Q31g: TDF and TDH both change keys on widening; only TDK1 keeps them |
179:| Q35c versioned channel | The stored key channel is named `decimal_order_v1`; a future encoding gets a new channel name and a migration. | R20 open question 3 |
180:| Q35d use constraints | TDK1 keys are never concatenated and never used for prefix queries (positive keys are not self-delimiting: `p0212` is a prefix of `p02125`); only whole-key equality and ordered comparison. | R20 open question 5 |
181:| Q35e errors and limits | Error names stay Q29d's (E-DECIMAL-OVERFLOW for a value beyond the declared or 38-digit precision, E-DECIMAL-FRACTION for excess fraction digits, E-DECIMAL-NARROWING, E-DECIMAL-BARE-DIVIDE); no E-DECIMAL-UNREPRESENTABLE. Maximum precision stays 38 (TDK1 has headroom to 99 integer digits). `-0.00` is refused (Q33b). No native XTDB DECIMAL lane: one verification path. If the kernel uses decimal.js or big.js, rounding tests must cover their signed zero. | R20; Q22, Q29d, Q33b |
