297:**Decimal (RULINGS Q18).** declaration: every decimal attribute declares precision p (total significant digits) and scale s (fractional digits), 0 <= s <= p. storage: every declared precision and scale (both lanes) -> the TDK1 key in channel decimal_order_v1 (authoritative, decodes exactly) plus derived canonical text at the declared scale; QLever also keeps its lexical channel for parity with other kinds (RULINGS Q35b, amending Q18). oneRepresentation: the decimal kind has exactly one stored representation; Q18's scaled-integer path no longer applies; money modeled as integer cents uses the integer kind (RULINGS Q35b). reject: a value with more than s fractional digits (counted on the value, so 16.500 fits scale 2, R-DEC-3) fails with E-DECIMAL-FRACTION; a value beyond the declared precision or 38 digits fails with E-DECIMAL-OVERFLOW; -0.00 in any spelling is refused with E-INVALID-VALUE (R-DEC-2); a decimal on an attribute not declared decimal is E-INVALID-VALUE (R-DEC-0); every rejection leaves no residue; never rounded or truncated (RULINGS Q33b, Q35e). wireShape: {"type": "decimal", "value": "16.50"} (R-DEC-1); a value with extra trailing zeros is returned at the declared scale (16.500 at scale 2 reads back as 16.50) while the original input text is still captured (Q22, R-DEC-3). toDecimal: toDecimal(number, p, s, rounding) starts from the number's exact binary expansion, then applies the caller's scale and rounding (R-DEC-6). avg: AVG over decimal is preflight E-DECIMAL-AVG unless written as divide(SUM, COUNT, scale, rounding) (R-DEC-7); decimal divide is modeled by the oracle (follow-up EXP-O03, R-DEC-5). family: decimal is its own family, ordered between number and datetime (CARD_MANY_ORDER); 1.50 and 1.5 are one value. comparison: CMP-DECIMAL-CROSS: comparison with integer or number is preflight E-MIXED-FAMILY unless the query converts explicitly. arithmetic: TypeScript kernel, exact decimal arithmetic; engine-side decimal arithmetic only where a projection needs it (RULINGS Q19). inputCapture: the writer captures the caller's original typed input before any normalization; tests assert nothing was rounded before capture (DEC-INPUT-CAPTURE, M-DECIMAL-PRECAPTURE-ROUND). kernelMaxDigits: 38.
321:| DS-WIDEN | a declaration change that admits every existing value is metadata-only: TDK1 keys depend on the value alone, so Q31g's key-compatibility condition always holds and no key is rewritten; stored values read back with the new declared scale (RULINGS Q35b, superseding Q31g's migration path) | DEC-SCALE-WIDEN |
