972:The expected outcome after a fault is chosen from the last transition with durable or engine-side evidence observed by the fault injector after the crash (the log file's records read back, the engine's committed transactions, the publication marker), never from the system's own claim. A transition the protocol declares but the receipt does not witness fails the case.
1033:A cursor or a requested historical basis issued before any transition below continues successfully afterward (through the exact ledger path where the projection changed), returning exactly the rows O-REPLAY computes at the cursor's basis. An available retained cut never becomes unavailable because its projection, mapping or index changed. No retention policy exists, so 'cut no longer retained' is never a permitted outcome.
1089:- Numbers inside the scenarios: pay rates, hours and money use integer cents (the integer kind) or the decimal kind (TDK1 key); the scenarios include the range rules 'pay rate at or above 16.50' and 'hours over 40' (cases GS-NUM-RANGE-1650 and GS-NUM-HOURS-40, selected by GS.18's dashboard filters) and at least one write of a value the lane cannot hold, which must fail with E-VALUE-OUT-OF-RANGE, E-DECIMAL-OVERFLOW or E-DECIMAL-FRACTION (GS-NUM-UNHOLDABLE, selected by GS.02).
1122:Counted separately: entities per tenant, domain facts, structural facts, assertion versions and retirements, config facts, journal, claim, receipt and feed metadata, maintenance relations, engine rows or triples by physical role, edges, presence events, corrections.
