     1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
     2	"""R20 property test for the recommended decimal order key ("TDK1").
     3	
     4	stdlib only.  Run:  python3 -I decimal_key_test.py [seed]
     5	
     6	What it checks (plain codepoint / unsigned-byte order only; no engine, no ICU):
     7	  1. Exhaustive: every value of several small (precision, scale) domains, in
     8	     ascending numeric order, gives strictly ascending keys, and decode(encode(v))
     9	     renders back to the exact canonical text with the declared scale.
    10	  2. Random pairs across (precision 1..38, scale 0..precision), including pairs
    11	     with DIFFERENT (precision, scale): sign(key_a ? key_b) == sign(Fraction_a ? Fraction_b).
    12	  3. Adjacent values (v and v + 1 ulp), carry boundaries (99..9 vs 100..0, 0.99..9 vs 1,
    13	     tiny values), domain min and max for every (p, s) up to p = 38.
    14	  4. Normalization: 1.1, 1.10, 1.100 (and any trailing-zero variant) share one key.
    15	  5. Decoder strictness / bijection: random strings over the key alphabet either fail to
    16	     decode or re-encode to themselves, so no two keys decode to the same value.
    17	  6. Negative zero is rejected, never normalized.
    18	  7. Mutation controls: five deliberately broken encoders must FAIL the same suite, so
    19	     the suite is shown able to fail.
    20	  8. Alternatives for the scale question (report Q3): TDF (fixed-width offset-binary,
    21	     key depends on precision and scale) and TDH (fixed-width integer part + stripped fraction,
    22	     key depends on integer digits only).  Both are order-correct inside one declaration;
    23	     the test shows which of them keep their keys when the declaration widens.
    24	"""
    25	import random
    26	import re
    27	import sys
    28	from decimal import Decimal
    29	from fractions import Fraction
