   202	## 5. Releases, hashing, diff and activation
   203	
   204	### 5.1 The release document
   205	
   206	```
   207	{"name": <"R1" or "R2">, "rules": [<rule entry> x 11], "world": "W0"}
   208	rule entry = {"id": <rule id>, "params": <object>}
   209	```
   210	
   211	**The eleven rule entries appear in this fixed order:** `LIFE`, `I9S1`, `I9S2`, `EVSUB`, `EVSTAT`, `BGC`, `DOCEXP`, `MINWAGE`, `OT`,
   212	`PAYCHG`, `PROP`. The order is the evaluation and trace order (section 8). One rule is one requirement.
   213	
   214	| Rule | Params | R1 | R2 |
   215	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   216	| `LIFE` | none | `{}` | `{}` |
   217	| `I9S1` | none | `{}` | `{}` |
   218	| `I9S2` | `businessDays` integer 1 to 10 | `{"businessDays": 3}` | `{"businessDays": 2}` (changed) |
   219	| `EVSUB` | `businessDays` integer 1 to 10 | `{"businessDays": 3}` | same |
   220	| `EVSTAT` | `tncBusinessDays` integer 1 to 20 | `{"tncBusinessDays": 8}` | same |
   221	| `BGC` | none | `{}` | `{}` |
   222	| `DOCEXP` | `leadDays`: object from document type to integer 0 to 365; the keys are the tracked types | `{"leadDays": {"ead": 0, "visa": 0}}` | `{"leadDays": {"ead": 90, "visa": 0}}` (changed) |
   223	| `MINWAGE` | `floorAttributes`: non-empty array of distinct location `cents` attribute names in ascending byte order | `{"floorAttributes": ["stateMinWageCents"]}` | `{"floorAttributes": ["localMinWageCents", "stateMinWageCents"]}` (changed) |
   224	| `OT` | `thresholdMinutes` minutes | `{"thresholdMinutes": 2400}` | same |
   225	| `PAYCHG` | `thresholdCents` cents | `{"thresholdCents": 500}` | same |
   226	| `PROP` | none | `{}` | `{}` |
   227	
   228	**R2 changes exactly three requirements:** the Section 2 due window (`I9S2`), the minimum-wage rule's location attributes
   229	(`MINWAGE`, which changes its read set), and the expiry handling of one document type (`DOCEXP`, `ead` only).
   230	
   231	### 5.2 Content hash
   232	
   233	`hash(release) = "sha256:" + lowercase hex of SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of canonicalJson(release)` (section 9). The hashes
   234	are in `releases/index.json`, and `validate.py` recomputes them. A release is immutable: changing a byte makes a different release.
   235	
   236	### 5.3 Release diff
   237	
   238	`diff(A, B)` is the list of rule ids, in release order, whose rule entries have different canonical JSON in A and in B.
   239	`diff(R1, R2) = ["I9S2", "DOCEXP", "MINWAGE"]`. This is the definition tier of R18 section 3.1 item 3: a sweep may skip a
   240	`(subject, rule)` pair whose rule is not in the diff and whose inputs did not change.
   241	
   242	### 5.4 Activation
   243	
   244	**Activation is a fact:** "this tenant uses release H from valid time F", committed at position `tx`. The activations passed to
   245	`resolveRelease` are the tenant's activation facts visible at the basis.
   246	
   247	`resolveRelease(activations, v)`:
   248	
   249	1. Candidates are the activations with `from <= v` (string comparison).
   250	2. No candidate: `W0.E_NO_RELEASE`.
   251	3. Pick the candidate with the greatest `from`; among equal `from`, the greatest `tx`; among equal `tx`, the greatest `release`
   252	   string (byte order).
   253	4. If the picked hash is neither R1's nor R2's: `W0.E_NO_RELEASE`. Otherwise return it.
   254	
   255	In-flight reconciles finishing on the old release is runtime behavior: each reconcile resolves the release once, at its own basis.
