   561	"Ascending" for strings is byte order of their ASCII bytes (equal here to Unicode code point order and to JavaScript's default
   562	string comparison, because every W0 string is printable ASCII).
   563	
   564	## 9. Canonical JSON
   565	
   566	**Every output, every release hash, and every work item id and version uses this one serialization.** Two implementations comply when their
   567	bytes are equal.
   568	
   569	1. UTF-8, no byte order mark, no whitespace outside strings, no trailing newline.
   570	2. Objects: members sorted ascending by key in byte order, written `{"k":v,"k2":v2}`.
   571	3. Arrays: elements in the order section 8.7 gives, written `[a,b]`.
   572	4. Strings: `"` + content + `"`, with `"` written `\"` and `\` written `\\`. W0 strings are printable ASCII, so no other escape occurs.
   573	5. Integers: decimal digits, `-` only for negatives (none occur in W0 outputs), no leading zeros, no `+`, no fraction, no exponent.
   574	6. Literals: `true`, `false`, `null`.
   575	
   576	Python produces it with `json.dumps(x, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"), ensure_ascii=True)`. JavaScript produces it with
   577	`JSON.stringify` over a copy whose object keys were inserted in sorted order, recursively. That recipe relies on a W0 fact: no
   578	object key in any W0 output or preimage is an array index such as `"7"`, which JavaScript would order first whatever the insertion
   579	order.
   580	
   581	## 10. Affected subjects (P3, GS.06b)
   582	
   583	`affected(release, worlds, writes)` returns the object `{"affected": [<subject>, ...]}`, listing ascending every subject s among
   584	`worlds` whose `dependencies` under `release`
   585	(computed from its own world as in 8.7) contain at least one pair of `writes`.
   586	
   587	1. **This is the expected affected-subject set** of `lab/TARGETS.md` 1.1 P3 item 3 for one transaction, with `worlds` at the basis
   588	   before the transaction and `writes` the `(entity, attribute)` pairs the transaction asserts or retracts.
   589	2. **Before-only is enough.** Every reference attribute (`location`, `documents`, `decisions`) is in the read set of some rule in
   590	   both releases. A transaction that changes which entity a subject references therefore writes a pair already in that subject's
   591	   dependencies, so the dependencies before the transaction catch every subject the after-state would add.
   592	3. **Direct writes are included.** Every placement, document and decision attribute is in some read set in both releases, so a
   593	   write to a subject's own facts always affects it.
   594	4. **The release matters.** Under R1 a write to `localMinWageCents` affects no one; under R2 it affects every placement at that
   595	   location (cases C35 and C36).
   596	5. **No valid-time filtering.** A correction to a past valid-time range still marks the subject; whether its current output changed
   597	   is left to the re-run.
   598	
   599	## 11. Golden cases
   600	
   601	### 11.1 File format
   602	
   603	```
   604	{
   605	  "id": "C01", "slug": "...", "title": "...", "op": "evaluate" | "reconcile" | "affected",
   606	  "checks": [{"rule": <rule id>, "polarity": "positive" | "negative"}, ...],
   607	  "identicalTo": <case id>      (optional: the expected bytes equal that case's),
   608	  "notes": [<derivation step>, ...],
   609	  "input": {...},
   610	  "expected": {...}
   611	}
   612	```
   613	
   614	**A conforming implementation** runs `op` on `input` and produces bytes equal to `canonicalJson(expected)`. `notes` are the hand
   615	derivation. A positive check means the rule emits at least one task, obligation, work item or flag in that case; a negative check
   616	means it emits none. The expected outputs were derived by hand from this file, not by a program.
